From 0e470180c9a1d59980dd64797b9d5888300f130a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Creitin Gameplays <clton999@outlook.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 14:41:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] mali: fix Synchronous External Abort in
 power_control_init; add MDBG probe instrumentation

The pristine DDK power_control_init() enables the DT clocks itself
(of_clk_get + clk_prepare_enable). On UMS9230 the first clock is
aonapb-gate#9 and clk_enable -> sprd_gate_enable -> regmap_read on the
AON APB bank raises a Synchronous External Abort (0x96000010), killing
the CPU - this was the cause of every custom-kernel boot hang (built-in
device_initcall and insmod alike). The vendor fork never runs this path:
the qogirl6 platform glue owns clocks and regulators via
mali_platform_init -> mali_power_on/mali_clock_on.

Skip the DDK clock/regulator handling when CONFIG_MALI_PLATFORM_NAME
contains qogirl6 and let the glue do the work. Verified on hardware:
insmod mali_kbase.ko -> Probed as mali0, Android completes boot with
surfaceflinger running (evidence in the analysis repo,
docs/evidence/2026-11-02-mali-abort-rootcause.txt / -fix-success.txt).

Also add optional probe instrumentation behind CONFIG_MALI_MDBG_CRUMB
(default n):
- mdbg_crumb.c: flash-persistent crumb recorder (miscdata+0xA0000,
  one byte per probe step, fsynced; survives any hang/reset) used to
  localize the abort when nothing else (console, pstore, page cache)
  survived the CPU stall.
- MDBG dev_info breadcrumbs + mdbg_crumb() calls at each dev_init[]
  step and every qogirl6 glue boundary (syscon lookups, nvmem, clock
  gets/enables, top-force write, pwr-state check, DVFS/QoS writes).
  Harmless dev_info-only output; keep for future bring-up work.
